# Shared devcontainer

This project's `.devcontainer/` is a thin layer over the shared one in
[pocket-nebula](https://github.com/aioue/pocket-nebula). Project-specific
devcontainer facts live in this repo's own rules, not here.

## What is editable

| Path | Editable here? |
|---|---|
| `.devcontainer/common/` | **No.** Vendored; overwritten on the next container create. Edit it upstream |
| `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` | Yes - project-specific keys only |
| `.devcontainer/site.env` | Yes - deployment suffix, version fallbacks, SSH aliases |
| `.devcontainer/site/` | Yes - optional `pre-setup.sh` / `post-setup.sh` hooks |
| `.devcontainer/common.ref` | Yes - which upstream ref to track |

`AGENTS.md` and this rule file are also generated. Edit `AGENTS.core.md`
upstream, or `.devcontainer/AGENTS.site.md` here.

## Why devcontainer.json is thin

`devcontainer.json` has no `extends` (devcontainers/spec#22, still open). Shared
mounts, extensions, settings, `containerEnv` and lifecycle hooks instead come
from the base image's `devcontainer.metadata` label, which the spec merges with
this file - **this file wins on conflict**.

Only what the spec cannot express in image metadata belongs here: `name`,
`build`, `runArgs`, `features`. Do not re-declare shared mounts or extensions
locally; `common/check-devcontainer-drift.sh` warns when you do, because a
duplicate silently stops tracking the image.

Lifecycle commands **concatenate** rather than override, so adding a
`postStartCommand` here runs in addition to the image's, not instead of it.

## Credential model

- Deployment selection is **deterministic per repo**, not interactive:
  `common/setup.sh` reads `OPENNEBULA_DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT` from
  `.devcontainer/site.env`. The interactive picker was removed on purpose -
  projects on this campus authenticate against the SAME OpenNebula instance, so
  a mis-selection silently operates as the wrong user instead of failing. Set
  `ONE_ALLOW_INTERACTIVE_SELECT=1` to opt back in
- Each deployment has two paired files in `~/.one/`: `one_auth_<SUFFIX>`
  (`user:password`) and `one_url_<SUFFIX>` (`ONE_URL` / `ONE_XMLRPC` /
  `ONEFLOW_URL` shell assignments)
- The `~/.one` bind-mount is **read-only** (the whole mount), so `setup.sh`
  copies the selected auth file to `~/.one_auth` and points `ONE_AUTH` there.
  The CLI reads `ONE_AUTH` when set, falling back to `~/.one/one_auth` only when
  unset
- **Do NOT keep a generic `~/.one/one_auth` on the host.** It is that fallback,
  and it may hold a different deployment's credentials - this misattributed a
  session once already. Keep only suffixed pairs so the fallback fails loudly
- Do NOT import OpenNebula credentials via `remoteEnv` `${localEnv:...}`. A host
  exporting another deployment's credentials, or an empty `ONE_AUTH`, would
  shadow the selected ones in every terminal: the CLI then fails with
  `ONE_AUTH file not present`, or worse, authenticates as the wrong user. Only
  `ANSIBLE_VAULT_PASSWORD` is legitimately host-provided

## Environment wiring

The selected credentials are written once to `~/.config/opennebula/env.sh` and
carried by **four** channels, each covering a failure the others do not:

| Channel | Covers |
|---|---|
| `/etc/profile.d/opennebula.sh` | Login shells. Survives devcontainer features regenerating `~/.bashrc`, which is why it is authoritative |
| `~/.bashrc` | Interactive non-login terminals, which skip `/etc/profile.d/` |
| `BASH_ENV` | Non-interactive agent shells |
| `/etc/environment` | Processes started with a clean environment. Without the `ONE_URL` pin, Ansible's OpenNebula inventory plugin fails with `Required config 'api_url' not provided` |

The file lives in the container home directory, deliberately **not** in
`.devcontainer/`: that is a bind mount from the host, so a plaintext password
there would land on the host filesystem inside a git repo, and `chmod 600` is
not reliably enforced across the mount.

## DNS

- `runArgs` set explicit `--dns`/`--dns-search` rather than bind-mounting
  `/etc/resolv.conf`. The bind-mount was unreliable: Docker Desktop on macOS can
  snapshot a partially-written `resolv.conf` after a VPN reconnect (e.g. a
  truncated `nameserver 172.16.1`), and the result is read-only with no fix
  possible from inside the container without `SYS_ADMIN`
- glibc honours only the **first 3** nameservers (`MAXNS`). A fourth entry is
  silently never queried, so the list is capped at three
- The internal resolver must stay **first**. glibc does not fall through on
  NXDOMAIN, so a public resolver first would answer NXDOMAIN for internal names
  rather than trying the internal one
- `runArgs` only take effect on rebuild. For the current session, apply a
  matching live edit to `/etc/resolv.conf`
- The devcontainer has the same network access as the host - Docker Desktop
  routes VPN traffic in. If something is unreachable the cause is almost always
  host VPN/DNS, not a Docker limitation

## Toolchain

- Do **not** put `PATH` in the base image's `containerEnv` metadata. It is passed
  to `docker run -e` literally, so `${PATH}` is not expanded and the entrypoint
  sleep loop fails with `sleep: not found`. Ansible entry points are symlinked onto
  `/usr/local/py-utils/bin` by `setup.sh`, which the python feature already puts
  on PATH at build time
- Python tooling installs with **uv**, not pip or pipx. Ansible and ansible-lint
  share one virtualenv (`/usr/local/ansible-venv`) so `ansible-core` is resolved
  once; entry points are symlinked onto `PATH`
- The venv is built on `/usr/bin/python3` with `--python-preference only-system`.
  Without that, uv downloads its own CPython and Ansible silently runs on a
  different interpreter from the one the editor settings pin
- Shell completions install to `/etc/bash_completion.d/` via
  `common/shell-completions.sh`, not the Dockerfile or `~/.bashrc`, so they
  survive feature regeneration - the same durability pattern as
  `/etc/profile.d/opennebula.sh`
- Dockerfile linting is the `docker.docker` (Docker DX) extension. hadolint, the
  binary and the extension, was removed: the extension had not been released
  since 2022

## OpenNebula CLI compatibility

`common/opennebula-cli-tools-patch.sh` installs stubs only when the installed gem
needs them, using a probe per stub rather than a version comparison, so each
retires itself. Verified against the published gems:

| gem | `load_opennebula_paths` | `HostSyncManager` |
|---|---|---|
| <= 7.2.0 | missing - stub needed | top-level require - stub needed |
| 7.2.1 | shipped (this is where OpenNebula/one#7608 landed, **not** 7.4.0) | still top-level - stub needed |
| >= 7.4.0 | shipped | lazy require behind `rescue LoadError` - no stub |

Do not probe `HostSyncManager` by requiring `one_helper/onehost_helper`: that
fails on 7.4.0 too, for unrelated reasons, so the stub would never retire. The
probe reads where the `require` sits in the installed gem instead.

## Versions

`common/detect-opennebula-version.sh` asks the live server and derives matching
CLI and PyONE specs. The `site.env` fallbacks apply **only** when the server is
unreachable; `*_VERSION_OVERRIDE` always wins. Note `~> 7.0` resolves to the
newest 7.x, not the server-matched patch.

Base image and action versions are kept current by Dependabot upstream, which
auto-merges everything except major bumps.
